Sleela Transition Protocol (STP) — Secured Pipe to SecureJDK 28

Document Series:    STP-0001
Revision:           1.0.0
Date:               September 2026
Status:             Normative for the v1 transition bridge
Endpoints:          Sleela (client)  <->  SecureJDK 28 Transition Supervisor (server)
Companions:         SLEELA.syntax (Sleela surface), userland/openjdk/jvm-config.xml
                    (SecureJDK 28 module configuration), userland/openjdk/SECUREJDK28.md

================================================================================
0. Purpose
================================================================================

The Sleela Transition Protocol (STP) is the wire contract by which a Sleela
program *transitions* its execution under the supervision of SecureJDK 28. A
Sleela process does not blindly run: before (and while) executing, it asks the
SecureJDK 28 Transition Supervisor to admit, monitor, and acknowledge its
memory model and parse, using SecureJDK's own security measures — class-load
guarding, integrity guardianship, the observer circuit (monitorability),
memory-proxy governance, and administrative supervision (see jvm-config.xml).

The transition is *secured with state-of-the-art cryptography* and carried over
a *local pipe* (a UNIX-domain socket by default) or, optionally, a *remote*
channel (TCP wrapped in TLS 1.3). If the supervisor acknowledges the parse in a
regioned, memorable, secured way, Sleela proceeds under supervision. If it does
NOT (deny, timeout, crypto failure, or supervisor unreachable), Sleela continues
LOCALLY as its own secure "safe trim", and the failed transition result is
persisted to a private, secured MySQL store for later review by an Admin.

This document fixes: the transport, the cryptographic envelope, the framing, the
message set, the region/acknowledgement model, and the failure/fallback rules.

================================================================================
1. Transport
================================================================================

1.1 Local pipe (default, "as local as its own secure safe trim").
    A UNIX-domain stream socket. Default path:
        $SLEELA_STP_PIPE  or  /run/sleela/stp.sock  (fallback: /tmp/sleela-stp.sock)
    The socket file MUST be owned by the launching user or root, mode 0600 or
    0660, never world-writable, never a symlink (checked on both ends). On a
    system without /run access the fallback path under the user's runtime dir
    is used.

1.2 Remote pipe (optional).
    A TCP connection to host:port (default port 8443) wrapped in TLS 1.3. The
    supervisor presents an X.509 certificate; the client pins the supervisor's
    long-term Ed25519 identity key (below) in addition to normal TLS validation
    (channel binding: the STP handshake transcript includes the TLS exporter
    value RFC 8446 §7.5, keyed with label "EXPORTER-STP-v1"). Selected with
        sleela run --secure --remote=host:port
    Remote is a strict superset: the same STP messages ride inside TLS.

1.3 The transport carries STP frames (§3). STP applies its own end-to-end
    cryptographic envelope (§2) EVEN on the local pipe, so a compromised pipe
    cannot forge or read a transition. On the remote path the envelope is
    nested inside TLS (defense in depth).

================================================================================
2. Cryptographic envelope (state of the art)
================================================================================

Primitives (all modern, all constant-time reference implementations):

    Identity / signatures     Ed25519            (long-term key per endpoint)
    Key agreement             X25519             (ephemeral ECDH per session)
    KDF                        HKDF-SHA-256
    AEAD (record encryption)   ChaCha20-Poly1305 (IETF, 96-bit nonce)
    Hash / transcript          SHA-256
    Random                     OS CSPRNG (getrandom / SecureRandom)

2.1 Long-term identities.
    Each endpoint owns an Ed25519 key pair. Public keys are exchanged out of
    band and pinned:
      - Sleela pins the supervisor's public key in $SLEELA_STP_SUP_PUBKEY
        (or /etc/sleela/supervisor.ed25519.pub).
      - The supervisor pins allowed client public keys in its authorized-clients
        set (jvm-config: transition.authorized-clients).
    An unpinned/unknown peer key is a hard failure (STP-DENY reason UNKNOWN_PEER).

2.2 Session handshake (a Noise-IK-style 1.5-RTT exchange).
      C -> S  ClientHello : { client_pub_ed25519, client_eph_x25519, nonce_c,
                              region_hint, proto = "STP-0001" }, signed by C.
      S -> C  ServerHello : { server_eph_x25519, nonce_s, region_grant,
                              policy_digest }, signed by S.
    Both sides compute:
        ss   = X25519(eph_priv, peer_eph_pub)
        th   = SHA-256( ClientHello || ServerHello )          (transcript hash)
        ikm  = ss  (|| TLS-exporter on the remote path)
        keys = HKDF-SHA-256(ikm, salt = th,
                            info = "STP-0001|c2s" / "STP-0001|s2c" / "STP-0001|region")
    yielding directional AEAD keys (k_c2s, k_s2c) and a 32-byte region key
    (k_region, §4). Each signature covers the running transcript hash, so the
    handshake is bound end-to-end and replay of a whole session is rejected by
    the fresh nonces.

2.3 Record layer.
    After the handshake, every frame body (§3) is sealed with the directional
    AEAD key. Nonce = 4-byte fixed salt (from HKDF) || 8-byte big-endian
    monotonic counter, per direction; the counter MUST NOT repeat under a key.
    The AEAD associated-data is the 8-byte frame header (§3.1). A decryption /
    tag failure aborts the session (reason CRYPTO_FAIL) and triggers fallback.

2.4 What "state of the art" buys us here.
      - Confidentiality + integrity of the transition even over an untrusted
        pipe (ChaCha20-Poly1305).
      - Mutual authentication and key pinning (Ed25519), so only an authorized
        Sleela talks to the real supervisor and vice-versa.
      - Forward secrecy (ephemeral X25519): capturing a pipe today does not
        decrypt a past transition if a long-term key later leaks.
      - Transcript binding + fresh nonces: no cut-and-paste / replay of acks.

================================================================================
3. Framing
================================================================================

3.1 Frame header (8 bytes, little-endian, sent in the clear; it is the AEAD
    associated data so it cannot be tampered with undetected):

        offset 0  u8   version        = 1
        offset 1  u8   type           (§3.2)
        offset 2  u16  flags          (bit0 = ENCRYPTED, bit1 = LAST)
        offset 4  u32  body_len       (length of the sealed body that follows)

3.2 Frame types

        0x01  HELLO_CLIENT     handshake (unencrypted, signed)
        0x02  HELLO_SERVER     handshake (unencrypted, signed)
        0x10  TRANSITION_REQ   sealed; the parse/memory-model submission (§5)
        0x11  ACK              sealed; regioned parse acknowledgement (§4)
        0x12  DENY             sealed; supervisor refuses (reason code)
        0x13  HEARTBEAT        sealed; observer-circuit liveness (monitorability)
        0x1E  CLOSE            sealed; graceful shutdown
        0x1F  ERROR            sealed or clear; protocol error (reason code)

3.3 Bodies are CBOR-like flat maps (documented per message). On the wire they
    are the AEAD-sealed bytes when flags.ENCRYPTED is set.

================================================================================
4. The region model — "regioned, memorable, secured" acknowledgement
================================================================================

A successful transition is acknowledged in a way that is (a) regioned — bound
to a named memory region the supervisor reserved for this program, (b) memorable
— carrying a stable, human-referable region id and mnemonic, and (c) secured —
authenticated with the region key so it cannot be forged or replayed.

4.1 Region.
    On admit, the supervisor reserves a supervised memory region for the Sleela
    memory model and returns:
        region_id     : u64        stable id (survives for the session)
        region_name   : text       e.g. "reg-emerald-0007" (the "memorable" name)
        region_class  : text       SecureJDK grade (Main/Manager/Archetype/...)
        capacity       : { ram_soft, ram_hard, ... } mirrored from memory-proxy
    region_name is a deterministic mnemonic derived from region_id via a small
    adjective+gem wordlist, so operators and Admin can refer to it by name.

4.2 Acknowledgement (ACK body).
        parse_ok      : bool       supervisor accepted the parse/memory model
        region        : { region_id, region_name, region_class, capacity }
        ack_tag       : 32 bytes   HMAC-style tag = HKDF/PRF(k_region,
                                    "ACK" || region_id || parse_digest)
        policy_digest : 32 bytes   SHA-256 of the applied module policy
        monitor       : { observer_endpoint, heartbeat_interval_ms }
    Sleela verifies ack_tag with k_region (from §2.2). A valid ACK with
    parse_ok = true means "proceed under supervision in this region." Anything
    else is a failure (§6).

4.3 Memorability at rest. region_id, region_name, parse_digest and ack_tag are
    what get recorded — on success in the supervisor's audit log, and on
    FAILURE in the MySQL admin store (§ MySQL) so an Admin can look the specific
    transition up by its memorable region name later.

================================================================================
5. TRANSITION_REQ — the parse / memory-model submission
================================================================================

Body fields:
        program_id    : text       stable id (e.g. sha256 of source path+mtime)
        source_name   : text       file name (for admin display)
        parse_digest  : 32 bytes   SHA-256 of the canonicalized Sleela parse
        mem_model     : {                       the Sleela memory model summary
            globals      : u32                  shared field slots
            functions    : u32
            code_len     : u32                  core instruction count
            max_threads  : u32                  requested (<= 128 core cap)
            locks        : u32
            mailboxes    : u32
            est_heap     : u64                  estimated bytes
        }
        constitution  : { sysdepth, degreemax } Sleela Constitution invariants
        want          : [ "monitorability", "admin", "supervision" ]  requested measures

The supervisor evaluates mem_model against its configured budgets (memory-proxy)
and grades (class-load-guard), applies the requested measures, and answers with
ACK (§4) or DENY (§6).

================================================================================
6. Failure & fallback ("continue as local secure safe trim")
================================================================================

6.1 A transition FAILS on any of:
        - DENY from the supervisor (reason: BUDGET_EXCEEDED, GRADE_DENIED,
          UNKNOWN_PEER, POLICY, ...);
        - CRYPTO_FAIL (bad signature, AEAD tag, or key pin);
        - TIMEOUT (no ACK within stp.timeout, default 3000 ms);
        - UNREACHABLE (no supervisor on the pipe / remote).

6.2 On failure Sleela does NOT abort. It continues LOCALLY as a "safe trim":
        - runs the program on its own C core (the normal path), but under a
          trimmed, conservative local policy: thread cap lowered to
          min(requested, safe_trim.max_threads = 16), print still atomic, and a
          local integrity note recorded;
        - marks the run "unsupervised (safe-trim)" in its output banner.
    The safe trim is the same deterministic core execution, just self-governed
    rather than JDK-supervised — "as local as its own secure, safe trim."

6.3 Every failed transition is written to the private secure MySQL store (see
    the MySQL Admin section) with: program_id, source_name, parse_digest,
    mem_model, the failure reason, the (attempted) region name if one was
    granted before failure, a UTC timestamp, and a status of NEW. An Admin later
    reviews, annotates, and resolves these rows. Persisting the failed result is
    best-effort and MUST NOT itself crash the safe-trim run (a store outage is
    logged locally and the run still completes).

================================================================================
7. State machine (client)
================================================================================

    IDLE --connect--> CONNECTING --hello--> HANDSHAKING --keys--> REQUESTING
    REQUESTING --ACK(parse_ok)--> SUPERVISED --run--> DONE
    REQUESTING --DENY/TIMEOUT/CRYPTO/UNREACHABLE--> FALLBACK
    HANDSHAKING/CONNECTING --error--> FALLBACK
    FALLBACK --safe-trim run + MySQL record--> DONE

The supervisor mirrors this: LISTEN -> HANDSHAKE -> EVALUATE -> {ADMIT+MONITOR |
DENY} -> CLOSE, emitting observer-circuit heartbeats while a region is live.

================================================================================
8. Versioning
================================================================================
The 1-byte header version and the "STP-0001" proto string gate compatibility.
A peer that does not speak version 1 receives ERROR(UNSUPPORTED_VERSION) and the
client falls back locally.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STP-0001 Revision 1.0.0 — the secured transition contract between Sleela and
SecureJDK 28. Local pipe by default; TLS 1.3 remote optional; modern AEAD +
Ed25519/X25519 throughout; regioned/memorable/secured acknowledgement; local
safe-trim fallback with MySQL admin capture on failure.
